    "description": "Iranian economic media provide reporting and commentary on markets, industries, public policy, and business. Publications such as Donya-e-Eqtesad and Tejarat-e Farda illustrate different editorial formats, while their reporting requires careful separation of sourced observations, interpretation, and forecasts.",
    "tags": ["Economic journalism", "Business media", "Financial reporting", "Donya-e-Eqtesad", "Tejarat-e Farda"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "needs_research",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-28T08:11:01Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-28T08:11:01Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Economic Media in Iran: Business Reporting and Policy Commentary",
      "description": "A guide to Iranian economic journalism, publication formats, editorial attribution, and careful interpretation of business and market reporting."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "economic-news-scope",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "From market information to policy debate",
        "content": "Economic media help readers navigate several distinct kinds of information: developments in individual industries, movements in markets, business decisions, and arguments about public policy. These subjects overlap, but they should not be treated as interchangeable. A price quotation describes a particular market observation; a commentary explains an author’s interpretation; a forecast expresses an expectation about the future.\n\nDonya-e-Eqtesad’s website illustrates this breadth through sections covering the economy, housing and urban affairs, securities markets, industry, mining and trade, banking and insurance, and international economic affairs. Its navigation also separates market-price material from editorial and analytical sections. Those categories describe the publication’s presentation, rather than independently establishing the accuracy of individual articles. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "formats-and-publishers",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Daily reporting and magazine analysis",
        "content": "Publication format affects how economic subjects are presented. Donya-e-Eqtesad identifies itself as a newspaper and provides a complete-issue link alongside website reporting. Tejarat-e Farda identifies itself as a weekly and offers an issue archive, print subscription, and digital-edition access. Its subject navigation includes political economy, social economy, economic thought, industry, energy, agriculture, and markets. These are useful starting points for distinguishing frequent news updates from issue-based discussion. [s1][s2]\n\nThe distinction is editorial rather than absolute. A newspaper can publish extended analysis, while a weekly can maintain a frequently updated website. Readers should therefore identify the actual article format and publication date rather than infer depth or reliability from frequency alone. The separate article and issue-access routes visible on these sites make that check especially worthwhile. [s1][s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "editorial-transparency",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Who is speaking?",
        "content": "Tejarat-e Farda’s About page identifies a license holder and lists editorial, analytical, translation, artistic, and technical roles. Such information helps readers distinguish an institution’s published identity from the authority of an individual contributor. A named economist’s interpretation remains an interpretation; an interviewee’s statement remains attributable to that interviewee. Neither becomes an official statistical finding merely because it appears in a specialist publication. [s3]\n\nThe same page links to Donya-e-Eqtesad, the English-language Financial Tribune, Eghtesad News, and EcoIran. These links provide discovery routes across related economic-media services. They are insufficient on their own to establish complete ownership relationships, editorial independence, or the operating condition of each linked outlet at the research cutoff. [s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "reading-economic-claims",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Reading an economic article carefully",
        "content": "A useful reading sequence begins by identifying whether an article reports an event, quotes a participant, interprets data, or predicts an outcome. For a numerical claim, follow the article to the original release where possible. Record the reporting period and unit before comparing figures: a stock, an annual flow, and a cumulative partial-year total answer different questions.\n\nMarket coverage needs equally precise handling. Donya-e-Eqtesad provides separate navigation for currencies, gold, and coins, demonstrating that even a single publication presents several price categories. A reader should retain the named instrument, quotation time, denomination, and market definition rather than reduce them to an unspecified headline price. [s1]\n\nEconomic media are most useful as guides to questions, arguments, and source documents. Their reporting can direct a reader toward evidence, but an article’s existence does not validate its underlying data or settle a policy dispute. This draft therefore presents publication formats and reading methods without ranking outlets or reproducing unverified market figures.",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      }
    ],
